WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.835
A useful alert tells the right reviewer about an event that may require a defined action.

00:00:04.835 --> 00:00:08.680
It should include camera, time, zone, event type, representative image or clip,

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.450
model version, confidence signal,

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:13.600
and enough preceding and following context for confirmation.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:15.950
Thresholds should reflect consequence and local conditions.

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:19.480
A lower threshold may catch more events while increasing false positives.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:21.860
Schedules, regions of interest, direction, duration,

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:25.760
and object size can make alerts more relevant without pretending uncertainty is gone.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:28.644
Group repeated frames and related detections into one event.

00:00:28.644 --> 00:00:32.840
Cooldown periods and escalation rules prevent a single ongoing condition from generating

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:33.981
dozens of notifications.

00:00:33.981 --> 00:00:38.200
Route routine review to a queue and reserve immediate alerts for cases with a clear,

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.720
time-sensitive response.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.340
Human review is essential before meaningful action.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:46.520
Reviewers should be able to confirm, dismiss, defer, add context, and escalate.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:48.470
When confidence is low or footage is unclear,

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:52.770
the system should communicate uncertainty rather than automatically assume the worst.

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:54.415
Document review decisions and outcomes.

00:00:54.415 --> 00:00:58.520
Feedback reveals confusing camera angles, recurring harmless activity, model drift,

00:00:58.520 --> 00:00:59.680
and rules that need tuning.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.400
Access to review queues and footage should follow role and privacy policy.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:07.120
Measure alert quality through confirmed relevance, missed events, review time,

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:10.320
duplicate rate, escalation outcome, and reviewer feedback.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.230
Alerting is successful when it supports timely,

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:16.270
accountable judgment without overwhelming people or turning uncertain model output into

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:17.048
automatic accusation.
